Table  I  summarizes the compositions of the three investigated conditions determined by OES. It becomes evident that the contents of Cr, Ni, Si, and Cu differ only marginally between the investigated conditions, whereas the relative variations in C and N are more pronounced. The wrought condition exhibits a 50 pct higher C content but, at the same time, a 50 pct lower N content compared to the HIP and PBF-LB/M conditions. The latter two do not differ significantly in composition, as both were produced from the same powder feedstock. Based on these compositions, the resulting  M d30  temperatures according to Nohara  et al. [ 18 ]  without considering grain size (equation is given in Eq. [ 1 ]) are − 54.3 °C (wrought), − 51.6 °C (HIP), and − 47.4 °C (PBF-LB/M), as listed in Table  I . It can be concluded that the chemical composition—and particularly the resulting  M d30  temperature—is similar and therefore comparable across all conditions. Moreover, it should be noted that the Wrought + SA condition, which according to the research hypothesis is expected to exhibit the highest fraction of deformation-induced martensite, actually shows the lowest  M d30  temperature and thus, based on its macroscopic chemical composition, the highest austenite stability. 1 M d3 0 = 551 - 462 ( pct C + pct N ) - 9.2 · pct Si - 8.1 pct Mn - 13.7 pct Cr - 29 pct Ni - 18.5 pct Mo - 29 pct Cu - 68 pct Nb - 1.42 ASTM-grain size-8 . 0 \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
